  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the main crystal structure types adopted by binary \\(M_2X_3\\) compounds, where the metal-like element \\(M\\) varies from As to Bi and the chalcogen \\(X\\) from S to Te. A phase map shows three dominant structure families—stibnite, tetradymite, and orpiment—occupying different compositional regions, highlighting how the preferred crystal phase changes systematically across the chemical series.\n\nThe central emphasis is on tetradymite, shown as a layered structure built from three distinct atomic positions labeled \\(X1\\), \\(M\\), and \\(X2\\). Next to it is a schematic Brillouin zone with marked high-symmetry points, indicating that this phase is important not only structurally but also for its electronic band structure. \n\nThe lower panels contrast the atomic arrangements of stibnite and orpiment. Stibnite appears as a more chain-like or ribbon-like network of linked atomic units, whereas orpiment shows a more extended layered motif. Together, the images convey that \\(M_2X_3\\) materials span several related but clearly different crystal architectures, with tetradymite standing out as a layered phase of particular interest.",
